**New Evidence**
- The strongest 2022-integrity signal is the press and scrutiny pattern around Erdoğan’s inner circle and family network.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Special Report-Insiders reveal how Erdogan tamed Turkey's newsrooms","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/special-reportinsiders-reveal-how-erdogan-tamed-turkeys-newsrooms-2883337","claim":"When Erdoğan's son-in-law quit as finance minister, newsroom managers told staff not to report it until the government said so, and pro-government media stayed silent for more than 24 hours, showing a chain of command tied to government-approved coverage.","locator":"lines 428-434","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters special report with direct interview-based reporting and explicit description of newsroom self-censorship around Erdoğan's inner circle.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but centered on a power-elite event whose media handling remained relevant in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High: the conduct concerns Erdoğan's inner circle, the government communications apparatus, and the president's family network.","contrary_evidence":["The story is based on interviews and media accounts, not a signed order from Erdoğan.","It describes silence and pressure, not a direct admission by Erdoğan."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Reuters Institute’s 2022 country report adds independent context on political pressure, ad favoritism, and takedowns.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey | Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism, Digital News Report 2022","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/turkey","claim":"The report says government organizations advertise almost exclusively in pro-government outlets, critical outlets are repeatedly fined by RTÜK, stories about abuses of government contracts are blocked or removed, and prominent independent journalists are detained or sued for reporting on issues not approved by the government.","locator":"lines 33-41","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent annual media research with explicit Turkey-specific findings and quantified audience/trust context.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and contemporaneous with the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is system-level pressure under Erdoğan's rule, not a single personal act, but it is still relevant to his tolerance of scrutiny.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that social media and some opposition outlets still provide space for independent coverage.","It is a country-level media environment report, not a personal bribery audit."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- A journalist was arrested after exposing a leak involving Erdoğan’s own ID card.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey to release journalist arrested over posts on personal-data leaks","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-04-28","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/04/28/us-turkey-security-journalist","claim":"The journalist said hackers had obtained confidential data from government websites and posted a partially obscured image of Erdoğan's ID; the Interior Ministry filed a complaint, asked prosecutors to bring charges, and the journalist was jailed pending trial before later release.","locator":"lines 653-670","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire content with clear chronology, official complaint, arrest, and release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High for the state response, though the underlying leak is a government-security issue rather than a direct personal-wealth issue.","contrary_evidence":["The journalist said he first contacted authorities and posted the leak as a warning.","He was later released, so the punitive response was not permanent."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

- Erdoğan’s party used a vaguely defined disinformation law against opposition criticism.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan rival is first target of Turkish media law, opposition party says","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-05","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/05/turkey-media-opposition","claim":"Parliament passed a law sought by President Erdoğan's ruling party that allows jail sentences for false or misleading information, and the main opposition said its leader became the first target after criticizing authorities over a drug 'epidemic'.","locator":"lines 663-669","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire with explicit attribution to the ruling party and the first enforcement target.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, shortly before the next election cycle.","ruler_attribution":"High: the law was sought by Erdoğan's party and was used against criticism of state conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed the law as a response to false information.","A legal challenge or prosecution is not itself proof that Erdoğan personally authored the complaint."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.8","7B.10"]}

- Erdoğan kept appointing loyalists into the economic chain of command.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan picks low-rates champion as finance minister as Turkish lira skids","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2021-12-02","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/erdogan-picks-lowrates-champion-as-finance-minister-as-turkish-lira-skids-2697129","claim":"Erdoğan appointed Nureddin Nebati, a strong supporter of the low-interest-rate line, as treasury and finance minister, replacing the last top official seen to favor orthodox policy, and Reuters noted he had already fired three central bank governors since mid-2019.","locator":"lines 423-434 and 442-447","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters directly ties the appointment to Erdoğan's control over the economic team and describes the loyalty/orthodoxy pattern.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited into 2022 and still governing in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"High: this is Erdoğan's appointment decision and part of his broader personnel control over economic policy.","contrary_evidence":["Nebati had prior government experience as a deputy finance minister.","Appointment alone does not prove personal enrichment, only loyalty and policy alignment."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.8","7B.10"]}

- A 2022 constitutional proposal looked tactical and reputation-managed rather than candid.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan moots putting Turkey headscarf reform to referendum","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/11/03/turkey-politics-headscarf","claim":"Erdoğan proposed putting a headscarf constitutional amendment to referendum, and Reuters reported that critics saw the move as an attempt to outflank the opposition, with the real target being the LGBT+ community rather than the stated women's-rights framing.","locator":"lines 659-667 and 673-687","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is solid on the statement and the political reading, though the opportunism inference is interpretive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to high: the proposal was Erdoğan's own public move, but the opportunism reading is partly interpretive.","contrary_evidence":["The proposal was formally framed as protecting women's right to wear a headscarf.","The issue had genuine rights-history context in Turkey, so the move was not purely fabricated."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.8","7B.10"]}

- The most serious family-network item is the 2023 Reuters complaint about Bilal Erdoğan, which points back to 2022 documents.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Special Report-US, Swedish prosecutors study graft complaint naming son of Turkey's Erdogan","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2023-06-26","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/special-reportus-swedish-prosecutors-study-graft-complaint-naming-son-of-turkeys-erdogan-3113279","claim":"Reuters reported that US and Swedish authorities were reviewing a complaint alleging kickbacks if Bilal Erdoğan helped a company win market access, that the plan would send tens of millions through a shell company and to institutions where Bilal was a board member, and that the company prepared a letter to President Erdoğan in June 2022 after contact with Bilal.","locator":"lines 422-437, 451-467 and 481-489","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters had documentary material, named denials, and official confirmation of preliminary probes, while clearly flagging the allegation status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 documents and conduct pathways.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to high for the family network; direct involvement by Erdoğan himself was not independently confirmed, but the route to presidential access ran through his son and family institutions.","contrary_evidence":["Reuters could not confirm that Erdoğan or Bilal were aware of or involved in the alleged scheme.","Bilal and a presidential spokesperson denied the allegations."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9","7B.10"]}

- A 2022 court ruling favored Erdoğan’s son-in-law against reporting that linked his foundation to public money.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkish court faults Birgün daily for report on Erdoğan’s son-in-law","publisher":"Turkish Minute","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.turkishminute.com/2022/05/18/sh-court-faults-birgun-daily-for-report-on-erdogans-son-in-law/","claim":"An Istanbul court ruled for Selçuk Bayraktar and his brother in a lawsuit over a report alleging they used millions of lira from TÜBİTAK's budget to organize TEKNOFEST through the T3 Foundation, and the plaintiffs said the report attacked their personal rights.","locator":"lines 206-214","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"A specific court outcome reported by a rights-oriented outlet; the underlying accusation remains contested, but the lawsuit itself is concrete.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is about Erdoğan's son-in-law and the family network, not a proven personal decision by Erdoğan himself.","contrary_evidence":["Birgün's lawyer said the report was lawful and within press freedom.","The article says a similar lawsuit had previously been rejected in Ankara."],"lenses":["7B.4","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"]}

- Another 2022 complaint by Erdoğan’s son-in-law targeted reporting on drone sales and family access.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Selçuk Bayraktar files for compensation against Şirin Payzın and Halk TV","publisher":"Expression Interrupted","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://www.expressioninterrupted.com/selcuk-bayraktar-files-for-compensation-against-sirin-payzin-and-halk-tv/","claim":"Erdoğan's son-in-law Selçuk Bayraktar filed a compensation lawsuit against a journalist and Halk TV over questions about Baykar drone sales during Erdoğan's UAE visit, after the program suggested the family was acting like sales representatives for the company.","locator":"search result snippet / page headline and summary text","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Press-monitoring outlet with a concrete lawsuit report; the snippet is specific but we did not get a full page open due verification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the suit is by the family network, but it is directly linked to Erdoğan's own foreign visit and the family's business profile.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a legal-complaint report, not a finding that the reporting was false.","The article describes a compensation claim, not a criminal finding."],"lenses":["7B.4","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 25-40 documents across Reuters, Reuters mirrors, Euronews/Reuters, Reuters Institute, Turkish Minute, Expression Interrupted, and a few broader governance/business pages.
- I kept items that had a direct personal-nexus angle: Erdoğan’s own statements, his son-in-law, the presidency’s media-control apparatus, legal retaliation against journalists, or documentary allegation trails touching Bilal Erdoğan and family-linked institutions.
- I rejected general Turkey corruption indicators, V-Dem/WGI/CPI context, and broad media-freedom rankings when they did not add ruler-specific conduct.
- I rejected some 2022 economic and political articles as too close to policy disagreement rather than integrity evidence, especially the interest-rate rhetoric items, unless they showed a personnel or scrutiny nexus.
- I excluded out-of-period material unless it directly documented 2022 conduct or 2022 documents. The 2023 Reuters Bilal complaint was retained only because it explicitly references June 2022 documents and meetings.
- I treated blocked/verification-gated pages as usable only when the search snippet itself carried the needed fact pattern.

**Remaining Gaps**
- I still do not have audited proof of Erdoğan’s own assets, gifts, or private financial enrichment in 2022.
- The strongest family-network evidence is still partly allegation-based, even when documented by Reuters and backed by preliminary probes or court filings.
- I have little direct evidence of Erdoğan admitting errors or correcting false claims in 2022.
- More search would help if you want a tighter answer on personal self-dealing versus family-network benefit, especially from primary legal/financial records or independent anti-corruption findings.
